"── 98_winui_browser.mst — WG5b-1: the Browser, over live reflection ─────
 docs/ROADMAP.md's WG5 row, second half, first slice. The four panes and the
 source pane, fed by the PRIMARY's live class hierarchy across the seam WG4
 D1 built.

 NOTHING NEW WAS NEEDED FOR THIS, and that is the point worth recording.
 `UiBrowserService browseSnapshot` already exists on the primary (34_tools);
 `Worker uiRequest: #refresh args: #(#browser)` already reaches it; and the
 reply already arrives as a nested tree of Strings, Arrays and smis. The
 snapshot is NAMES ONLY by construction — the pickle REFUSES class objects,
 and that refusal is what enforces `no class oop crosses a VM boundary`
 rather than a convention anyone has to remember.

 So this file is GUI wiring over a model it does not own, which is the same
 sentence `66_cocoabrowser.mst` opens with, and the same rule §2.3 states for
 every view: *consumed, not re-implemented*.

 WHAT THIS SLICE IS NOT. It is READ-ONLY. Accept, New Class, New Method and
 Remove all write to the image through `image_store::flows` — the web GUI's
 own implementation — and that needs a host service Windows does not have
 yet. It is WG5b-2, it is where the CG8 gate lives (*Accept persists
 byte-identically to the web path*), and rushing it to make this look
 complete would be exactly the wrong trade: a browser you can read is most of
 what a browser is for, and a write path that ALMOST matches the web's is a
 corruption bug with a UI in front of it.

 THE FOUR PANES, and where each comes from:

   packages   the top-level groupings — here, the hierarchy's own top level
   classes    the classes within, flattened depth-first with indentation
   protocols  instance / class side (the Smalltalk-80 pair, not a category
              model — the snapshot carries no categories, and inventing them
              would be the browser lying about the image)
   selectors  the chosen side's selectors, alphabetical (the snapshot's order)
   source     the method's source — IMAGE data, so WG5b-2; today it shows the
              selector and says where its source will come from, rather than
              showing a plausible-looking reconstruction."

Object subclass: WinShell [

    "── the model, fetched once per refresh ──────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> refreshBrowser [
        "ONE request for the WHOLE tree, not one per pane. The snapshot is a
         full-hierarchy traversal on the primary, and four panes each asking
         for their own slice would be four traversals of the entire image to
         draw one screen."
        Worker uiRequest: #refresh args: (Array with: #browser) onReply: [ :reply |
            WinShell browserTreeArrived: reply ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserTreeArrived: reply [
        "The reply is #(#browserTree <node>) on success, or #(#uiPending ...)
         when the primary has no UiBrowserService — which is a real state, not
         an error: a world booted without the tools layer has no hierarchy to
         project."
        ((reply isKindOf: Array) and: [ reply size >= 2 and: [ (reply at: 1) == #browserTree ] ])
            ifFalse: [ ^self appendTranscript: 'browser: primary has no snapshot to give' ].
        BrowserTree := reply at: 2.
        BrowserRefreshes := (BrowserRefreshes isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ BrowserRefreshes ]) + 1.
        self rebuildBrowserPanes.
        "WG6a: the Outliner renders the SAME tree, so it fills from the same
         arrival. A no-op until 104 defines it, and a no-op whenever the
         Outliner has never been opened — there is nothing to rebuild."
        self rebuildOutlinerIfBuilt.
        "WG6c-3: and the Editor's class picker, from the SAME snapshot. Three
         views, one traversal of the image — which is the reason this arrival
         is the fan-out point rather than each view asking for its own."
        self rebuildEditorClassesIfBuilt.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserTree ^ <Object> [ ^BrowserTree ]
    WinShell class >> browserRefreshes ^ <Integer> [
        ^BrowserRefreshes isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ BrowserRefreshes ] ]

    "── walking the snapshot ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     Node shape, fixed by UiBrowserService and quoted here so a reader of this
     file need not go and find it:
       {name. instVars. classVars. instSelectors. classSelectors. children}"

    WinShell class >> nodeName: n <Array> ^ <String> [ ^n at: 1 ]
    WinShell class >> nodeInstVars: n <Array> ^ <Array> [ ^n at: 2 ]
    WinShell class >> nodeClassVars: n <Array> ^ <Array> [ ^n at: 3 ]
    WinShell class >> nodeInstSelectors: n <Array> ^ <Array> [ ^n at: 4 ]
    WinShell class >> nodeClassSelectors: n <Array> ^ <Array> [ ^n at: 5 ]
    WinShell class >> nodeChildren: n <Array> ^ <Array> [ ^n at: 6 ]

    WinShell class >> flattenTree: node <Array> depth: d <Integer> into: out <OrderedCollection> [
        "Depth-first with the depth kept, so the classes pane can INDENT rather
         than pretend the hierarchy is a flat list. Indentation is the whole of
         how a Smalltalk browser shows inheritance in one column."
        out add: (Array with: (self nodeName: node) with: d with: node).
        (self nodeChildren: node) do: [ :c | self flattenTree: c depth: d + 1 into: out ].
        ^out
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserRows ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        BrowserTree isNil ifTrue: [ ^OrderedCollection new ].
        ^self flattenTree: BrowserTree depth: 0 into: OrderedCollection new
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserClassCount ^ <Integer> [ ^self browserRows size ]

    WinShell class >> nodeNamed: aName <String> ^ <Object> [
        self browserRows do: [ :r | (r at: 1) = aName ifTrue: [ ^r at: 3 ] ].
        ^nil
    ]

    "── the selected path ────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> browserSelectedClass ^ <Object> [ ^BrowserClass ]
    WinShell class >> browserSelectedSide ^ <Symbol> [
        ^BrowserSide isNil ifTrue: [ #instance ] ifFalse: [ BrowserSide ] ]
    WinShell class >> browserSelectedSelector ^ <Object> [ ^BrowserSelector ]

    WinShell class >> selectBrowserClass: aName <String> [
        BrowserClass := aName.
        BrowserSelector := nil.
        self rebuildSelectorPane.
        self updateSourcePane.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> selectBrowserSide: aSymbol <Symbol> [
        BrowserSide := aSymbol.
        BrowserSelector := nil.
        self rebuildSelectorPane.
        self updateSourcePane.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> selectBrowserSelector: aName <String> [
        BrowserSelector := aName.
        self updateSourcePane.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserSelectors ^ <Array> [
        | node |
        BrowserClass isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array new: 0 ].
        node := self nodeNamed: BrowserClass.
        node isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array new: 0 ].
        ^self browserSelectedSide == #class
            ifTrue: [ self nodeClassSelectors: node ]
            ifFalse: [ self nodeInstSelectors: node ]
    ]

    "── the panes themselves ─────────────────────────────────────────────
     Four SysListView32s in report mode (§2.1's Panes row) plus the source
     EDIT. Built lazily with the view, like every other content child."

    WinShell class >> browserPaneNames ^ <Array> [
        ^Array with: #browserClasses with: #browserProtocols
               with: #browserSelectors with: #browserSource
    ]

    WinShell class >> buildBrowserPanes [
        "Called from `ensureViewBuilt:` for #browser. The four panes share the
         content rect, split by `browserPaneRects` — a pure computation, so it
         is testable with no window exactly as the shell's own layout is."
        self browserPaneNames do: [ :n |
            n == #browserSource
                ifTrue: [
                    "WG5c D3: the source pane is the other code surface, and
                     it is the one Accept reads — so it becomes a RichEdit
                     alongside the Workspace, by the same `codePaneClass`."
                    self addControl: n class: self codePaneClass text: ''
                        style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                            bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VSCROLL')))
                            bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'ES_MULTILINE')
                                bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'ES_AUTOVSCROLL')))
                        exStyle: 0 ]
                ifFalse: [
                    self addControl: n class: 'LISTBOX' text: ''
                        style: (((WinApi constant: 'WS_CHILD')
                            bitOr: ((WinApi constant: 'WS_BORDER') bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'WS_VSCROLL')))
                            bitOr: (WinApi constant: 'LBS_NOTIFY'))
                        exStyle: 0 ] ].
        "Every pane needs a font, and a LISTBOX needs the SHELL's font rather
         than the bar's. Measured: with the custom 9pt bar font the list held
         175 items (LB_GETCOUNT said so) and drew none of them — an empty white
         box that looks exactly like a fill that failed and is not one. With
         `guiFont` — the system UI font the shell already uses for its standard
         controls — the same list draws immediately.

         Which is also the RIGHT font by §2.1: the bar's is for the custom-drawn
         strip, and standard controls under visual styles wear the system's."
        self browserPaneNames do: [ :n |
            (self controlNamed: n) ifNotNil: [ :c |
                n == #browserSource
                    ifTrue: [ self prepareCodePane: c ]
                    ifFalse: [ c setFont: self guiFont ] ] ].
        "Position them NOW, for the same reason a content child is positioned
         at build time: a view switch does not relayout (WG4 D6 — every content
         child shares one rect, so a switch changes no geometry), which means a
         pane built after the last layout would never be given a position and
         would show as an empty content area. Measured exactly that way, twice
         now — once for content children, once here."
        self isOpen ifTrue: [
            | rects |
            rects := self layout
                rectsIn: self clientWidth
                by: self clientHeight
                dpi: (Dpi isNil ifTrue: [ WinApi constant: 'USER_DEFAULT_SCREEN_DPI' ] ifFalse: [ Dpi ]).
            self layoutBrowserPanes: (rects at: #content) ].
        self refreshBrowser.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserPaneRects: content <Array> ^ <Dictionary> [
        "PURE: the content rect in, four pane rects out. The classic
         Smalltalk-80 arrangement — three list panes across the top third, the
         source filling the rest — and the arithmetic is here rather than in a
         layout object because these four are a UNIT that only exists together."
        | x y w h top out third |
        x := content at: 1. y := content at: 2. w := content at: 3. h := content at: 4.
        top := h * 35 // 100.
        third := w // 3.
        out := Dictionary new.
        out at: #browserClasses put: (Array with: x with: y with: third with: top).
        out at: #browserProtocols put: (Array with: x + third with: y with: third with: top).
        out at: #browserSelectors put: (Array with: x + (third * 2) with: y with: w - (third * 2) with: top).
        out at: #browserSource put: (Array with: x with: y + top with: w with: h - top).
        ^out
    ]

    WinShell class >> layoutBrowserPanes: content <Array> [
        | rects |
        rects := self browserPaneRects: content.
        self browserPaneNames do: [ :n |
            (self controlNamed: n) ifNotNil: [ :c | c moveTo: (rects at: n) ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> showBrowserPanes: aBoolean <Boolean> [
        self browserPaneNames do: [ :n |
            (self controlNamed: n) ifNotNil: [ :c | c show: aBoolean ] ].
        ^self
    ]

    "── filling them ────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> listSet: aName <Symbol> items: items [
        | c |
        c := self controlNamed: aName.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        c send: (WinApi constant: 'LB_RESETCONTENT') wParam: 0 lParam: 0.
        items do: [ :s |
            c send: (WinApi constant: 'LB_ADDSTRING')
                wParam: 0
                lParam: (WinArena scratchUtf16: s) ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> rebuildBrowserPanes [
        "Indentation carries the hierarchy — two spaces per level, which is what
         a Smalltalk browser has always used to show inheritance in one column."
        | names |
        names := OrderedCollection new.
        self browserRows do: [ :r |
            | pad |
            pad := String new.
            1 to: (r at: 2) do: [ :i | pad := pad , '  ' ].
            names add: pad , (r at: 1) ].
        self listSet: #browserClasses items: names.
        self listSet: #browserProtocols items: (Array with: 'instance' with: 'class').
        self rebuildSelectorPane.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> rebuildSelectorPane [
        self listSet: #browserSelectors items: self browserSelectors.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> updateSourcePane [
        "WG5b-2 makes good on the promise this method used to print: source is
         IMAGE data, and it now comes from the image.

         The read/write split is the Mac's, deliberately (66_cocoabrowser's own
         note): the hierarchy and selector ROWS come from the primary's live
         reflection, but TEXT comes from `image_store` — so what the user edits
         is what the next boot will read, rather than a rendering of what this
         session happens to hold.

         A selector with no stored source is not an error. It happens for
         anything compiled live this session and never Accepted, and the pane
         says so plainly instead of showing an empty box that looks like a
         failed read."
        | c text |
        c := self controlNamed: #browserSource.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        text := self sourceTextFor: BrowserClass selector: BrowserSelector.
        c setText: text.
        ^self
    ]

    "`sourceTextFor:selector:` is NOT here, and the reason is a compile-time
     one worth stating: it names WinHost, which 99 defines — and 99 must load
     AFTER this file, because its own Accept reaches the selection accessors
     above. A class name is resolved when the method is compiled (unlike a
     message send, which is not), so the two cannot both be satisfied and the
     method moves to 99 rather than the load order bending around it."

    WinShell class >> isBrowserPane: aName <Symbol> ^ <Boolean> [
        ^(self browserPaneNames indexOf: aName) > 0
    ]

    WinShell class >> onBrowserPaneChanged: aName <Symbol> [
        "Which row, asked of the control rather than remembered — the list is
         the authority on its own selection, and a shell-side mirror of it
         would be one more thing to get out of step."
        | c i |
        c := self controlNamed: aName.
        c isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        i := c send: (WinApi constant: 'LB_GETCURSEL') wParam: 0 lParam: 0.
        i < 0 ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        aName == #browserClasses ifTrue: [
            | rows |
            rows := self browserRows.
            (i + 1) > rows size ifTrue: [ ^self ].
            ^self selectBrowserClass: ((rows at: i + 1) at: 1) ].
        aName == #browserProtocols ifTrue: [
            ^self selectBrowserSide: (i = 1 ifTrue: [ #class ] ifFalse: [ #instance ]) ].
        aName == #browserSelectors ifTrue: [
            | sels |
            sels := self browserSelectors.
            (i + 1) > sels size ifTrue: [ ^self ].
            ^self selectBrowserSelector: (sels at: i + 1) ].
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> browserVariables ^ <Array> [
        "The snapshot DOES carry variable names, so the variables the browser
         shows are live rather than pending — unlike source, which is image
         data and waits for WG5b-2."
        | node |
        BrowserClass isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array new: 0 ].
        node := self nodeNamed: BrowserClass.
        node isNil ifTrue: [ ^Array new: 0 ].
        ^self browserSelectedSide == #class
            ifTrue: [ self nodeClassVars: node ]
            ifFalse: [ self nodeInstVars: node ]
    ]
]
